{
  "dom_id": "step-q253831",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14162617/assets/7879f99e6dd09e7ada4c1d920b78504df70bf0c511380ea21728a755d6590d23.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $2.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le 2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>2.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,2]=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=2\\notin A.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
